f the  ways that that scientists imagine that  you get variants like this where you  accumulate a lot of variance in one  strain  is likely with the evolution within a  single patient who had  a in a long  infection and most likely this person  was probably immunocompromised so that  they had an infection for 20 weeks or  longer  that allows the variance that allows the  the evolution of the virus to take place  in all kinds of um  different ways within one host and  that's how you can probably accumulate a  large  number of mutations like this from in a  single genome  anyway back to the back to the story all  three of these  variants have those same kinds of  patterns of large numbers of mutations  and in fact which is really interesting  and kind of a a testament to the concept  of convergent evolution  is that all of these five variants of  concern  share the d614g mutation and  and a few others are shared by by three  or four of the  of the variants of concern so even  though this virus has been out in the  world  in millions of people millions of hosts  with all kinds of opportunities to  evolve  in unlimited numbers of ways essentially  at the end of the day  the variants that are causing us most  concern do  in some ways at least look very similar  to one another  so how do we know all this um  information um  a lot of research has been done on the  virus using  various methods in next generation  sequencing  these are an overview of some of the  workflows that uh  that we have worked on at illumina um  this this is an rna virus  so you can essentially do meta  transcriptomics or shotgun  rna-seq on a nasal swab and you can see  the virus and that's this workflow at  the top here  or you can use enrichment um think of  like exome sequencing or probe enhanced  enrichment of the of the virus that  works very well and we have a we have a  series of products  uh that can be used to study that virus  and in addition other viruses that's the  sort of middle workflow there  and we break it down by the library prep  kit the type of sequencer that is used  and then the type of analysis tool that  is used for each of these workflows  but the um the method i'm going to talk  about the most today is the amplicon  method  this is a method that we developed into  a in  an eua an fda approved eua test we  called it the kobitsek test  and more recently we have made an ru  version of this  uh protocol to be used for surveillance  and i'm going to talk about some of the  details of that  we've also created lots of different  work um dragon pipelines dragon is our  as our informatics pipeline  methods uh and that that i'll get into  more details here in a second  but um we have several now interesting  dragon pipelines for analyzing  code so as i mentioned  the kovitsik test originally um  it's interesting we started working on  the covet seek test  now they think about it about a year ago  um  in r d and illumina we sort of dropped  everything and started working on this  covet seek test  and within two months we had an fda  approved eua for this test and products  on the shelf and everything uh to uh  to give to customers to to study the  virus the test itself and the eua  approval is actually for the diagnostic  answer of yes or no do you have the  virus or not  the sequencing of the virus is sort of a  an additional benefit of using this  um since then um there are there are  groups that use this for  uh detection only but since then many  more groups are interested in studying  variants and studying the genome  sequence and so we have tweaked the  protocols that i'll talk about a little  bit  in an ru version of this that will give  even better  genomes than the original kovitsik test  which was more tweaked for  detection and sensitivity and speed  so the assay itself i'll get into some  of the details  this is the the assay the outline of the  assay is very similar for the eua  and the and whether you're running it in  the rule mode or the eua mode  it is based upon 98 amplicons it  controls  a bit tile across the virus it also has  11 human  rna controls and it's designed to run  anywhere from 384  all the way up to 3072 samples in a  single run  the biochemistry plus process of the  workflow is like i said very similar  between  both the eu the eua and the rule version  but essentially it starts with um a  sample  that is rna extracted converted to cdna  and then amplified using the covet seek  primers and down there at the bottom  you can see we used two pools of primers  these are based upon the arctic  designs that are uh are out there in the  community  the the pool one and pool tool are  separated so that they don't interact  with each other and that you can get  overlapping amplicons during the pcr  process  so the green uh pool two primers and the  red  pool one primers are in separate tubes  you amplify up the virus  after up to slides up the cdna then you  pull them back together  and then what we do in our process is we  then essentially shotgun sequence each  one of those amplicons using a  transposome method  to fragment them randomly into a kind of  a shotgun pool of of  sequences across every amplicon and then  we sequence it at very high depth on a  novaseq  and we use that data to ascertain  uh whether the virus is detected or not  and to get the sequence  this process can be scaled up quite  high on a on a novaseq and this is the  way the math works out  we have four sets of index plates so  that's four times 96 that gives you 384  different indexes that can be used on  the system  each set of 384 can be loaded into one  lane  so one pool per lane there are four  lanes on each flow cell  and then the the system can run two flow  cells at a time so that's  96 times four 384 times four and then 15  36  times two gets you up to three thousand  and seventy two samples per run  and the original um kovid seek test  we were going for speed and so we were  doing a very fast  1 by 36 base pair run that run can be  completed and analyzed in in about 12  hours  uh for surveillance as as we'll talk  about we have extended the read lengths  to two by 100 or two by  150 this gives you more genome coverage  and actually more complete genomes as  we'll talk about  so this is some of the performance from  the covet seek test and this shows you  how sensitive  sensitive the test is and and how  in the original version of the test that  uses 36 base pair  uh single reeds we still were able to  get genomes out of quite a lot of the  um of the of the samples so  essentially this test has a cutoff here  that lower red line  that if we detect at least five of the  90 amplicons 98 amplicons  and we detect them with more than 10  reads per amplicon we call that a  a presence and that's the presence of  the virus  if we detect more than 90 of the  amplicons then we reported out a  sequence consensus sequence in the  original test  and that region in between or five to 90  amplicons detected  was um that was deemed positive for  covalent positive for the virus but  not not a consensus sequence and so you  can see that sort of starts to fall off  between ct values of 30 and 35  between 30 and 35 we still get a as  at least some of the genome out and  that's well above our detection limit  what we have learned since the original  test is that if you're going to use this  biochemistry in this assay for um  for um surveillance we have a few tweaks  to the assay that help give it better  genome coverage  the first one is is that it's it's now  known that if you  if you do the same pcr process but with  a 63 degree annealing temperature  uh compared to a 65 degree annealing  temperature in the pcr step  that gives more uniform coverage and  better coverage  and so you can see that um that's what's  shown here this is an example of 16  samples  that were compared in in terms of the  percentage of non-n  bases so essentially what percentage of  the genome was covered 