n uh  version of the covet lineage app here in  the next few weeks  so this app like i said is much more  flexible it allows you to actually  tinker with the thresholds of for for  the detection thresholds and also for  the sequencing  metrics so you can you can you can  uh adjust coverage thresholds uh virus  detection threshold  virus negative threshold human control  thresholds you can  you can change those any way you want if  you're using it  for detection in an ruo sense  um it also allows you to basically  create  um fasta files  from the sequences that can then be used  in all kinds of interesting analysis so  as i said if you look on the left here  you can take the output of the kovid  seek  app or just fast q files from  any uh illumina sequencing run that has  gone from bcl to fastq conversion  or from other applications that we have  that i didn't talk about today but like  for instance our  our our respiratory virus oligo panel  which is our uh kind of a  pan respiratory virus has up to 30 or 40  different viruses that can be detected  there  if you have data from that and and there  are sars reads in there  you can take those fastq files and put  them through this  app as well and so  uh this app creates a fast a file  which is the genome sequence and then  that kind of under the hood  runs a series of of other applications  for you  that are open source tools that you we  can kind of  utilize that that many people around the  world are using these tools right now to  kind of characterize  the virus during uh surveillance also  these fafsa files can be used if you  look at the  lower arrow there for uh sharing data  sharing like submission to jizzaid or  sra submission or sra import so i'm  going to talk a little bit about some of  the details of these  so this is the the lineage app summary  report and it's a little bit different  than our original um  kovitsek uh test app uh summary report  in this  in this um we you know like i said most  of the time the the samples that go  through this  are known to be positive so it's more of  a question of what is the lineage  and what kind of variants are found here  so  one of the handy things that this does  is it can take all of your samples  and instead of having to load them one  at a time into the  to the lineage report you can  upload them as a concatenated fasta file  which is  quite uh quite useful it also has a link  to next strain where you can upload it  directly to nextdrain  it it shows you the lineages that are  assigned by this pangolin tool  it gives you probabilities of that the  version of the pangolin tool the  pangolin tool is updated  at least weekly these days with new data  and and and sometimes new lineages  it gives you some qc information of that  and it kind of tells you the percent of  genome that it's covered that went into  that analysis  here's an example for instance just to  prove um oh i want to go back one slide  one thing i wanted to highlight here if  you look at the  the five data types that are putting in  we're putting in here  um none of these are actually the  uh the classic coded seek data  these are all examples of other types of  of assays  that have created data fast queue files  that have been loaded into this  into this coped lineage app and analyzed  this way  so like i said this this app can analyze  data from  any application any any library prep and  any read length  and give you out this kind of  information  here's an example for instance this is  an rna-seq so this is like  shotgun meta transcriptome of a nasal  swab  and you can see here's the fasta file  that was created by the coverage lineage  app here on the right  and it will also uh show you a coverage  plot  of how well the 29 000  plus genome of the virus is covered and  you can see we have  excellent in this particular example  5400  average medium coverage or median  coverage so  this is just an example of one of the  outputs  the other thing it will do is